Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 6.2. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (f) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, the Company Board may change its recommendation if, and only if, (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, that an Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal, (ii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board’s intention to make such a change not earlier than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the fourth Business Day after Parent receives such notice, and (iii) the Company shall have negotiated in good faith with Parent during such notice period to make adjustments to this Agreement so that the Acquisition Proposal would no longer constitute a Superior Proposal. Furthermore, the Company Board shall consider, in addition to the financial terms, the likelihood of regulatory approvals, the timing of the transaction, and the potential impact on stakeholders, in determining whether an Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal. (Pages 54-55)\nAnswer:
Choices:

